WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:11.920
On the night of the 20th of January 1936, just&nbsp;
before midnight, King George V of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:20.320
Kingdom died at Sandringham House. His eldest son&nbsp;
was present in the royal residence and immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:28.880
succeeded him as King Edward VIII. The following&nbsp;
morning, Edward broke with royal protocol by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.280
being present at St James’ Palace to watch from a&nbsp;
balcony as the proclamation of his own accession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:44.080
as king was read out to the nation. Previous&nbsp;
monarchs had not done this. It was the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.280
small sign that Edward would refuse to adhere to&nbsp;
the basic rules of the monarchy. An even greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:01:00.960
problem though, was the woman by his side on the&nbsp;
21st of January 1936. This was Wallis Simpson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:09.280
a twice-married American divorcee whom Edward was&nbsp;
in a relationship with. Over the next two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:16.160
Edward’s love life and his inflammatory behaviour&nbsp;
would lead to him losing his throne and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:23.200
entering into a highly controversial tour of&nbsp;
Nazi Germany where he met with the likes of Adolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:32.480
Hitler, Hermann Goring and Joseph Goebbels. All&nbsp;
the while, Wallis Simpson was by his side after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:41.280
they finally married each other on the 3rd of&nbsp;
June 1937 in France. Why did Edward have to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:49.040
up his throne for Wallis? Were he and his wife&nbsp;
traitors who engaged in talks with the Nazis about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:57.360
restoring Edward to the throne in Britain? This is&nbsp;
the story of King Edward VIII and Wallis Simpson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:10.880
the king who abdicated and the woman&nbsp;
for whom he gave up his throne. 
 
&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:17.360
The man known to history as King Edward VIII
of the United Kingdom and Emperor of India
&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:25.680
was born on the 23rd of June 1894 at White
Lodge, Richmond Park in Surrey, England.
&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:32.880
His father was George, the eldest son of Edward,
Prince of Wales, the son and heir of Queen
&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.680
Victoria of Britain.
As Victoria was into her mid-seventies by
&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:44.480
the time young Edward was born in 1894, it
was clear that her son, the future Edward
&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.920
VII, Prince of Wales, and Edward’s grandfather,
would soon succeed her.
&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.760
That would place the young Edward as second
in line to the throne when it occurred, which
&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.040
it soon did when Victoria died in January
1901.
&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.200
Young Edward’s mother was Mary of Teck,
the daughter of the Duke of Teck, a senior
&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.440
German.
Between them George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.080
and Mary had six children,
five boys and one girl.
&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.600
Edward was the eldest, but&nbsp;
nearly as consequential
&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:25.280
as the years went by was the next eldest child,
a boy named Albert after his great-grandfather,
&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.680
Victoria’s long deceased husband over who’s
death she had never fully recovered.
&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:41.360
Edward’s full name was Edward Albert Christian
George Andrew Patrick David and during his
&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.920
youth he was always referred to within the
family as David.
&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.880
Edward was raised from his very youngest years
as a future king.
&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.400
He would, no doubt, not ascend to the throne
for several decades, but accidental deaths
&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.680
and illnesses had created a situation where
a person in line to the throne in the way
&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.560
Edward was could sometimes ascend at a very
young age.
&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.160
His parents were aloof and somewhat gruff
in their parenting methods, but it was not
&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.920
a wholly unhappy household, though Edward
grew to become wary of his father’s angry
&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:24.400
outbursts about relatively unimportant issues.
He later stated in his memoirs that he felt
&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.280
unloved and his childhood experiences seem
to have inculcated in him a desire to avoid
&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.520
having children in later life, which he never
would.
&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.440
More broadly, Edward became known for having
an easy charm in his younger years, which
&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.280
allowed him to mix freely with members of
different classes, though his intellect was
&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.320
hardly prodigious.
In these younger years he and his siblings
&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.560
were largely educated at home at York Cottage
at Sandringham and at Frogmore near Windsor
&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.440
Castle.
As he entered his teenage years Prince Edward
&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.577
was sent to the naval college at Osborne on
the Isle of Wight.
&nbsp;

00:05:07.577 --> 00:05:07.667
From there he progressed through to the Royal
Naval College and then on to serve on HMS
&nbsp;

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:10.640
Britannia.
This was a virtually identical training to
&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.920
that which his father had undertaken in his
younger years and which indeed has remained
&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:22.400
a staple of royal princes ever since.
Despite being an heir to the throne, Edward
&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.600
was not overly protected and experienced some
bullying in his youth in the navy.
&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.760
Otherwise, his upbringing was somewhat limited.
He was not trained to develop his mind or
&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.800
become a significant scholar in the same way
in which his forebears in the seventeenth
&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.400
or eighteenth centuries might have been.
As a result he grew up with an intellectually
&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.120
limited worldview.
This limited intellectual development was
&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:55.680
all the more concerning when in May 1910,
with the death of his grandfather, his father
&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.320
became King and so Edward became heir to the
throne at the age of fifteen.
&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.000
According to tradition, he was soon given
the title of Prince of Wales and despite still
&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.160
being a teenager was quickly drawn into public
life.
&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.240
The occasional appearances at public events
were interspersed throughout the early 1910s
&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.960
with studies at the University of Oxford which
his father had decided Edward should attend.
&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.600
However, Edward proved an indifferent student
and when turmoil struck Europe towards the
&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.400
end of his time there he was&nbsp;
glad of the distraction.
&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.680
In the summer of 1914 war descended across
Europe.
&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.480
It had been brewing for decades as the rise
of a united Germany in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.080
1870s had destabilised
the balance of power in Central Europe and
&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.600
created a major rival to Britain.
Other issues such as rivalry for colonial
&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.120
possessions in Africa and fervent nationalist
sentiment in the Balkans, where the Empire
&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:09.280
of Austria-Hungary and the Russian Empire
were rivals to secure control over the collapsing
&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:16.000
Ottoman Empire, had compounded matters.
In the end it was a regional crisis here in
&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.480
the summer of 1914, the assassination of the
heir to the Austro-Hungarian Empire, Archduke
&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:29.040
Franz Ferdinand, by a Serb nationalist, which
lit the match that ignited the war.
&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.400
In the final days of July and the first week
of August Britain, France and Russia went
&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.800
to war with Germany and the Austro-Hungarian
Empire.
&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.800
The major front for the British for the duration
of what soon became known as the First World
&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:51.360
War was in northern France where the British
and French were soon bogged down in attritional
&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.160
trench warfare with the Germans.
As part of the effort by the royal family
&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.200
to show solidarity with the millions of British
men who were now being conscripted into the
&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:07.440
military and sent to fight in France, Prince
Edward and others were assigned to serve as
&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.320
officers in the army.
Edward was commissioned into the Grenadier
&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:19.520
Guards in the last days of July 1914 and took
to military service very well, finding that
&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.720
he enjoyed the camaraderie in ways which his
studies had not fulfilled him.
&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.760
However, his wartime experience can hardly
be said to have been authentic.
&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:36.080
Neither Edward, nor any other senior members
of the royal family or major noble lines could
&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.720
be placed in harm’s way, where they might
be captured or killed.
&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:45.600
As such for much of the next four years he
was effectively chaperoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.760
by his fellow soldiers
in roles across northern France.
&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.040
Some of these were tokenistic, such as when
he was sent as a sort of royal ambassador
&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.000
to meet with French generals, but when he
appeared to inspect British army camps on
&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.840
the Western Front it is understood to have
genuinely improved morale on the front.
&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:11.520
Here was a prince and a member of the royal
family actively showing up to do his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.040
military
service.
&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:19.200
Indeed, on one or two occasions, despite the
extensive precautions taken, Edward did find
&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.560
himself in danger during the war, notably
when his chauffeur was killed by exploding
&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.480
artillery and his car crashed in northern
France.
&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.520
Moreover, his range of activities extended
beyond France, with a visit to the Middle
&nbsp;

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:40.160
East in 1916 to meet and greet Britain’s
Australian and New Zealander allies.
&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:46.400
The war was significant in one other way which
would have a small implication for Edward
&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.760
and his family for decades to come.
At the outset of the conflict the royal family
&nbsp;

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:55.120
was known as the House of Saxe-Coburg and
Gotha.
&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:00.960
This had been established in 1901 following
the succession of King Edward VII, bringing
&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.640
the House of Hanover which had ruled Britain
for nearly two centuries to an end.
&nbsp;

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:12.080
However, the House of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha
name, which was assigned on account of the
&nbsp;

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.200
extensive links between the British royal
family and many of the most senior royal lines
&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:22.640
within Germany, became problematic in the
context of the First World War.
&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.800
It reminded far too many people that the royals
had extensive amounts of German blood and
&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.480
indeed George V was the first cousin of the
German Kaiser, Wilhelm II.
&nbsp;

